sg-mail (0.1.0-6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Microsoft accounts through DavMail (sg-davmail, a new dependency):
    mail, calendars and contacts of Outlook.com, Hotmail, Live and
    Microsoft 365 accounts, read and written, with one sign-in in DavMail's
    own window -- no Thunderbird release reads or writes Microsoft calendars
    yet. File > Add Account (and the first start) asks the address first: a
    Microsoft one (its domain, or MX at Microsoft 365) is connected through
    DavMail (Thunderbird's IMAP and SMTP account, CalDAV calendar and
    CardDAV address book on the gateway); any other, or "Use Thunderbird's
    account setup", goes to Thunderbird's own setup with the address typed
    in. An account Thunderbird set up itself (EWS, Graph, IMAP at
    Microsoft) is offered "Calendar and contacts (Microsoft)" (also
    Calendar > Add calendar).
  * The gateway: one per account, the systemd user service
    sg-mail-davmail@NAME (/usr/lib/sg-mail/sg-mail-davmail), on 127.0.0.1
    only, for that address only, settings and DavMail's token private
    (0700/0600); the service only uses DavMail's stored token and never
    opens a sign-in window; the sign-in is a short-lived DavMail of its own.
    /usr/bin/sg-mail starts the gateways. The Calendar's side pane says when
    one is not running (Start), needs DavMail's sign-in again (Sign in
    again) or cannot reach Microsoft. Removing the account removes its
    gateway, token, calendar, address book, passwords and outgoing server.
  * Works on Debian's Thunderbird 140 as on 157 (an incoming server's host,
    password removal: both versions' interfaces).
  * A new account's Inbox is shown once its server has listed it.
  * Open Shared Calendar through a Microsoft account's gateway (DavMail's
    /users/THEIR-ADDRESS/calendar/): the address kept as the server writes
    it (Thunderbird 140 wrote %40 and then showed none of the calendar's
    events); such calendars go with the account. The Scheduling Assistant's
    server free/busy reaches DavMail's CalDAV outbox (Graph getSchedule);
    Automatic Replies stay client-side for these accounts (DavMail does not
    offer Exchange's).
  * Gates: davmail (a DavMail stand-in in front of Radicale: offer, sign-in,
    user service, appointments created, dragged and deleted, contacts,
    Start, Sign in again, Cancel, removal), davmail-mail (the whole account
    through DavMail: mail in, mail out, one Sent copy, calendar, contacts),
    davmail-service (the real DavMail: its sign-in window and Cancel, the
    service on the loopback only, "Sign in again"); all on 140 and 157,
    with 18 mutants, all killed. The test root (root-tb-r3) has DavMail's
    runtime and systemd;
    build/inroot.sh: SG_NONET=lan (a network card that leads nowhere) and
    SG_STAGE (the packages laid over /usr); make stage. look: SG Mail's
    own account step before Thunderbird's setup. launcher: the gateways
    started (mutant).

